      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To study frequency and extent of meningeal enhancement in patients with and without post-lumbar puncture headache (PLPH) with cerebral MR with Gd-DTPA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "Ten consecutive patients with PLPH and 9 consecutive patients without PLPH were included in the study. Nine of the PLPH patients were reinvestigated when the PLPH was over and all non-PLPH patients during the week after the lumbar puncture."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Eight of the 9 patients with PLPH showed more enhancement of meningeal structures during PLPH than when PLPH had disappeared. The differences were slight in 5, more marked in 2, and pronounced in 1 patient. The 9th patient showed no change at all. In the 9 patients without PLPH, there was mainly slight enhancement before lumbar puncture and no change in enhancement after lumbar puncture as compared with before."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "PLPH is related to increased Gd-DTPA enhancement of the meninges, although the increase in enhancement during PLPH is minor in most cases. Pronounced enhancement of the meninges after lumbar puncture may predict long duration of the PLPH."
        }
      ]},
